<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="ch-scale-exercises" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Scale Exercises</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      This chapter presents a set of melodic scale exercises based on the Cmaj7 tonal centre. 
      Each exercise trains a different intervallic pattern — diatonic thirds, triads — and 
      should be practised slowly before increasing tempo. Once secure in C major, transpose 
      to all twelve keys.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-scale-exercise-cmaj7-in-thirds">
    <title>Cmaj7 — In Thirds</title>
    <p>
      The following exercise moves through the C major scale in diatonic thirds, ascending 
      then descending. Practise with a steady pulse; every pair of notes should be even and 
      connected.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-scale-exercise-cmaj7-in-thirds">
      <caption>
        Cmaj7 scale exercise in thirds — ascending and descending in diatonic thirds 
        over a C major tonal centre.
      </caption>
      <image xml:id="img-scale-exercise-cmaj7-in-thirds"
             source="notation/scale-exercise-cmaj7-in-thirds"
             width="100%" />
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-scale-exercise-cmaj7-in-triads">
    <title>Cmaj7 — In Triads</title>
    <p>
      This exercise extends the pattern to diatonic triads. Each group of three notes outlines 
      a triad built from successive scale degrees. The exercise ascends through two octaves and 
      descends back to the root.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-scale-exercise-cmaj7-in-triads">
      <caption>
        Cmaj7 scale exercise in triads — diatonic triads ascending and descending over a 
        C major tonal centre.
      </caption>
      <image xml:id="img-scale-exercise-cmaj7-in-triads"
             source="notation/scale-exercise-cmaj7-in-triads"
             width="100%" />
    </figure>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-scale-exercise-cmaj7-variation">
      <title>Variation</title>
      <p>
        The following variation applies a descending triad pattern starting from the upper 
        register. The groupings move downward through each chord tone before stepping to 
        the next scale degree.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-scale-exercise-cmaj7-variation">
        <caption>
          Cmaj7 scale exercise in triads — descending variation.
        </caption>
        <image xml:id="img-scale-exercise-cmaj7-variation"
               source="notation/scale-exercise-cmaj7-variation"
               width="100%" />
      </figure>
    </subsection>
  </section>

</chapter>
